Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport.

---

### **Program.cs** — stdio host with DI

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: route all logging to stderr so stdout stays clean for JSON-RPC
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("api", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer(options =>
{
    options.ServerInfo = new() { Name = "RestApiServer", Version = "1.0.0" };
    options.ServerInstructions = "Use these tools to query the REST API.";
})
.WithStdioServerTransport()
.WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### **ApiTools.cs** — tool class with constructor DI

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using System.Text.Json;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a post by its numeric ID.")]
    public async Task<string> GetPost(
        [Description("The post ID (1-100)")] int id,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching post {Id}", id);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        var post = await client.GetFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>(
            $"/posts/{id}", cancellationToken);
        return post.ToString();
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("List all posts by a specific user.")]
    public async Task<string> GetPostsByUser(
        [Description("The user ID to filter by")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        var posts = await client.GetFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>(
            $"/posts?userId={userId}", cancellationToken);
        return posts.ToString();
    }

    [McpServerTool(ReadOnly = false, Destructive = false),
     Description("Create a new post.")]
    public async Task<string> CreatePost(
        [Description("Author user ID")] int userId,
        [Description("Post title")] string title,
        [Description("Post body text")] string body,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        var response = await client.PostAsJsonAsync("/posts",
            new { userId, title, body }, cancellationToken);
        var result = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
        return result;
    }
}
```

---

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class | Services in the constructor (like `ApiTools` above) |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services as method params alongside tool params |

**Key rule:** The SDK automatically distinguishes tool parameters from injected services. Types registered in DI (`HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<>`) are **resolved automatically** and **never appear** in the tool's JSON schema that the LLM sees. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.

So in `GetPost` above, the LLM schema only shows `id` — the logger and HTTP client are invisible to it.

**To scaffold this project from scratch:**

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n RestApiServer
cd RestApiServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
```

Then replace the generated files with the code above, build, and run:

```bash
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The process will block on stdin waiting for JSON-RPC messages — that's the expected stdio behavior. Connect it from VS Code, GitHub Copilot, or test with `npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector`.
